a bare \code{sfc} column is worse: a \code{POINT} column writes \emph{silently} as \code{DOUBLE[]},
and other geometry types abort with a message naming neither column nor type.
Convert to text or to GeoArrow WKB first, and take one of the routes above;
a \code{wk_wkb} column is no shortcut outside Arrow, because it writes as \code{BLOB} like any other list of raw vectors, dropping its class and its CRS.
The duckspatial and duckdbfs packages wrap this.
\item \strong{A \code{geoarrow_vctr} column writes its indices, silently.}
The \code{geoarrow_vctr} that \code{as.data.frame()} gives for a geometry in an Arrow result
is an integer vector of indices into the Arrow data it holds.
\code{dbWriteTable()} of it, and \code{\link[DBI:dbWriteTableArrow]{dbWriteTableArrow()}}, which converts through such a data frame,
write those integers as an \code{INTEGER} column, \verb{1, 2, 3}, without an error.
Convert it with \code{sf::st_as_sfc()} first, or keep it in Arrow.
}
